  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 306 PM MST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nThursday...Another relatively mild day is expected with strong \nsouthwest flow aloft and the paucity of low and mid level moisture. \n700 mb temperatures near 3 Celsius will help produce maximum \ntemperatures in the mid 40s to lower 50s. Low level gradients \nsuggest a breezy day.\n\nFriday...Even stronger southwest flow aloft prevails with a decent \nshortwave trough aloft moving across central Wyoming, producing \nsynoptic and orographic snows for Carbon County and the Snowy and \nSierra Madre Ranges, and dry conditions elsewhere. Breezy to windy \nbased on low and mid level gradients.\n\nInterestingly, this date, the 20th of November marks the latest \nfirst measurable snowfall of 0.1 inches or greater in the autumn at \nCheyenne, and it looks as if we will likely eclipse this record, \nwith no measurable snowfall at Cheyenne through November 20th. \n\nSaturday...The split shortwave trough aloft moves across the Rockies \nwith drying conditions noted for our counties in the afternoon.\n\nSunday...Ridging aloft moves overhead with westerly flow aloft over \nour counties. Again, the atmosphere looks too dry for any \nprecipitation development.\n\nMonday...In this progressive pattern, the next shortwave trough \naloft moves into western Wyoming, spreading orographic snows across \nour Snowy and Sierra Madre Ranges, though amounts look minimal, with \ndry conditions elsewhere.\n\nTuesday...As the shortwave trough aloft moves across early in the \nday, expect scattered rain and snow showers, with drying noted in \nthe afternoon as a low amplitude shortwave ridge aloft transitions \noverhead.",
